1
00:00:20,557 --> 00:00:26,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the Sabbāsava Sutta, Majjhima Nikāya II.

2
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:33,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Just a few sections that I want to speak about.

3
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:40,064
[SPEAKER_00]: In the first section, the basic summary of it.

4
00:00:42,107 --> 00:00:51,012
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, because I say that the destruction of the taints is for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know and see.

5
00:00:54,874 --> 00:00:57,335
[SPEAKER_00]: And, who knows and sees what?

6
00:00:59,076 --> 00:00:59,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, yeah.

7
00:01:00,416 --> 00:01:04,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It's translated as wise attention and unwise attention.

8
00:01:04,737 --> 00:01:06,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he knows the difference there.

9
00:01:06,978 --> 00:01:07,358
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows.

10
00:01:07,458 --> 00:01:07,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

11
00:01:08,278 --> 00:01:12,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that wise attention is yoniso manasikāra.

12
00:01:13,899 --> 00:01:22,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And for now, I will translate that as an attention with perspective.

13
00:01:24,542 --> 00:01:26,142
[SPEAKER_00]: So, who knows and sees what?

14
00:01:28,723 --> 00:01:29,483
[SPEAKER_01]: You could probably say.

15
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:34,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's yoniso of manasikāra, yoniso manasikāra.

16
00:01:34,891 --> 00:01:36,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could say perspective of attention.

17
00:01:37,713 --> 00:01:43,756
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know perspective of attention and non-perspective of attention.

18
00:01:43,897 --> 00:01:44,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

19
00:01:46,418 --> 00:01:50,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not the attention necessarily that is different.

20
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when you have perspective on your attention, you don't have perspective.

21
00:01:53,882 --> 00:01:56,304
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're aware, as we say peripherally,

22
00:01:57,325 --> 00:02:02,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Regarding what you're attending in front of you, or you're not aware, peripherally, regarding what you're attending in front of you.

23
00:02:02,827 --> 00:02:08,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, that's exactly, that's, that's by definition, that's what perspective is.

24
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:18,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Having perspective means not being absorbed in, like, one narrow, one pointed thing, but actually having perspective on it, not losing the context of that, of that thing, of that situation.

25
00:02:22,178 --> 00:02:23,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see why that's necessary?

26
00:02:23,338 --> 00:02:28,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Because often in the suttas, we would give those similes of the trap, the poison drink, all that.

27
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:32,042
[SPEAKER_01]: And all those things can only be known if you gain perspective in regard to it.

28
00:02:32,542 --> 00:02:36,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because nothing you attend in the trap will reveal the trap as a trap.

29
00:02:37,364 --> 00:02:38,304
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a trap.

30
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:45,367
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you know, if you have perspective that it's a trap, then you will basically not engage with it.

31
00:02:45,747 --> 00:02:49,429
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have perspective that it contains poison that you cannot detect, but you know

32
00:02:51,183 --> 00:02:54,984
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is perspective in regard to attending to this poisonous drink.

33
00:02:56,325 --> 00:02:56,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not drink it.

34
00:02:57,305 --> 00:03:06,368
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I said there is no single thing that would bring sensuality, engaging with your senses, to diminish as proper attention, as perspective of attention.

35
00:03:06,508 --> 00:03:07,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Yoniso manasikāra.

36
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:13,050
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's not a single thing that would make sensuality to increase as not having the perspective.

37
00:03:13,310 --> 00:03:14,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Ayoniso manasikāra.

38
00:03:15,911 --> 00:03:16,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can...

39
00:03:20,744 --> 00:03:26,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Being lustful towards a sensual image.

40
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:26,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

41
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:34,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective in regard to that would be, well, all the effort that you put in practicing the dhamma that then has revealed, um,

42
00:03:35,055 --> 00:03:39,560
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense objects to be perilous, dangerous, resulting in death, suffering.

43
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:41,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not something you always have to...

44
00:03:41,662 --> 00:03:42,803
[SPEAKER_01]: I have to re-attend now.

45
00:03:43,144 --> 00:03:44,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's perilous.

46
00:03:44,145 --> 00:03:44,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

47
00:03:44,545 --> 00:03:47,248
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes part of the perspective, if you've been doing it rightly.

48
00:03:47,729 --> 00:03:53,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you attend to sensuality, as the Buddha would say, the danger is apparent.

49
00:03:54,515 --> 00:04:00,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when perspective becomes apparent, it means your yoniso, manasikāra has been properly fulfilled.

50
00:04:00,299 --> 00:04:02,340
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gaining knowledge and wisdom.

51
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:03,521
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the bigger picture.

52
00:04:03,681 --> 00:04:03,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

53
00:04:04,522 --> 00:04:12,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it takes effort to be focusing on the perspective, or to be discerning the perspective, while the focus is taken by sensuality, by attention here and there.

54
00:04:13,968 --> 00:04:20,412
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you invested enough of that peripheral effort, peripheral development, so to speak, then things become apparent.

55
00:04:20,813 --> 00:04:24,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, when I attend to sensuality, all I see is the danger.

56
00:04:25,298 --> 00:04:31,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if I attempt to non-sensuality, all I see is peace and pleasure, pleasure of renunciation.

57
00:04:31,881 --> 00:04:33,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes effort to develop.

58
00:04:40,725 --> 00:04:43,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have a perspective on what you are attracted to?

59
00:04:46,827 --> 00:04:47,308
[SPEAKER_00]: True.

60
00:04:47,348 --> 00:04:50,370
[SPEAKER_00]: The perspective on the nature of the body.

61
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,291
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

62
00:04:51,311 --> 00:04:55,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see it, those things, on the level of perspective.

63
00:04:55,534 --> 00:04:58,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Not make another thing you're attending of.

64
00:05:08,391 --> 00:05:10,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Because either way, you're unaware of perspective.

65
00:05:10,854 --> 00:05:12,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how people do their subha wrongly.

66
00:05:13,738 --> 00:05:14,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see beautiful.

67
00:05:14,659 --> 00:05:15,540
[SPEAKER_01]: I must see ugly now.

68
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:16,441
[SPEAKER_01]: I must see ugly now.

69
00:05:16,501 --> 00:05:23,390
[SPEAKER_01]: But that will work initially, maybe, when kind of the reality, the surreal kind of aspect of ugly hits you.

70
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:24,271
[SPEAKER_01]: But you get used to it.

71
00:05:24,771 --> 00:05:25,893
[SPEAKER_01]: It ceases to be surreal.

72
00:05:27,047 --> 00:05:28,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you haven't developed perspective.

73
00:05:28,468 --> 00:05:37,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's when, in the vinaya pitaka, like, all those rules that revolve around, like, basically, people being attracted to... becoming attracted to ugly, and breaking rules, and so on.

74
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:40,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they haven't had perspective to begin with.

75
00:05:41,114 --> 00:05:48,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to practice a subha rightly, it's in regard to the beautiful, you want to see the perspective as revealing the ugly underneath.

76
00:05:48,958 --> 00:05:52,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Not make another thing you look at, and with your attention, and stare at.

77
00:05:52,979 --> 00:05:55,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, either way, there will be no perspective.

78
00:05:57,078 --> 00:06:04,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if you look in the world, there's people who are attracted to even the most disgusting things.

79
00:06:04,304 --> 00:06:08,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Even, you know, excrement is attractive for them.

80
00:06:08,768 --> 00:06:09,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

81
00:06:09,088 --> 00:06:09,768
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there are.

82
00:06:09,808 --> 00:06:11,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's all... because there's no perspective.

83
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:12,471
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

84
00:06:13,932 --> 00:06:21,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how... Whatever is in front of you, has potential of becoming attractive, because you haven't uprooted sensuality.

85
00:06:21,929 --> 00:06:25,492
[SPEAKER_01]: You're craving for pleasure, and you're not developing perspective.

86
00:06:25,512 --> 00:06:29,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just have it long enough, sufficiently enough, and you'll be attracted to it.

87
00:06:30,717 --> 00:06:35,561
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do have perspective, even the most beautiful and attractive things will actually be seen as ugly.

88
00:06:36,142 --> 00:06:40,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are truly ugly and repulsive.

89
00:06:41,687 --> 00:06:44,209
[SPEAKER_01]: If they weren't so, sensuality would not be unwholesome.

90
00:06:44,569 --> 00:06:46,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality would not be a contradiction.

91
00:06:47,136 --> 00:06:47,996
[SPEAKER_01]: It would not be pain.

92
00:06:48,036 --> 00:06:49,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It would not be a cover-up.

93
00:06:49,097 --> 00:06:52,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It would not be, basically, something you you could get rid of.

94
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:07,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when one attends with a perspective, unarisen taints arise...

95
00:07:08,186 --> 00:07:09,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Without a perspective.

96
00:07:09,186 --> 00:07:09,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

97
00:07:09,406 --> 00:07:14,728
[SPEAKER_00]: When one attends without a perspective, unarisen taints arise and arisen taints increase.

98
00:07:17,606 --> 00:07:29,462
[SPEAKER_00]: When one attends with a perspective, or has... one has perspective on one's attention, what one is tending to, unarisen things do not arise, and arisen things are abandoned.

99
00:07:29,542 --> 00:07:30,704
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you just said.

100
00:07:32,284 --> 00:07:37,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I think what I was thinking of was, I think it was that, it was in Anguttara, Samādhi Sutta, but it says the same.

101
00:07:37,667 --> 00:07:38,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It says the same.

102
00:07:40,428 --> 00:07:41,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Then that's, that's the whole point.

103
00:07:41,869 --> 00:07:45,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you realize it's, it's the, it's the, it's the fundamental.

104
00:07:45,951 --> 00:07:49,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, right from the start, whether you're going to be going in the right direction or the wrong direction.

105
00:07:51,463 --> 00:07:53,144
[SPEAKER_01]: With perspective is the right direction.

106
00:07:53,945 --> 00:07:55,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Without perspective is the wrong direction.

107
00:07:55,265 --> 00:08:06,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you if you start without a perspective on the right level, no amount of your practice, then, can sort of change the fact that you took the first step in the wrong direction, and now that's where you're heading.

108
00:08:07,693 --> 00:08:16,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where you've been meditating with sensuality, not free from sensuality, not really getting any distinction of the kind that matters.

109
00:08:16,978 --> 00:08:19,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's why yoniso manasikāra is one of the

110
00:08:20,932 --> 00:08:23,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Prerequisites for becoming a Sotāpanna.

111
00:08:23,453 --> 00:08:26,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a necessary basis.

112
00:08:26,194 --> 00:08:30,455
[SPEAKER_01]: If a Putrajana would have... Obviously, perspective is not a static thing.

113
00:08:30,495 --> 00:08:32,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people have more, some people have less of it.

114
00:08:32,075 --> 00:08:37,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people are wise and natural, some people are not, because they, you know, the life made them so, they made the effort, whatever.

115
00:08:37,757 --> 00:08:38,817
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally,

116
00:08:39,763 --> 00:08:41,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You need more of a perspective.

117
00:08:41,464 --> 00:08:44,946
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how lofty, elevated, putrūjana you are or not.

118
00:08:45,286 --> 00:08:46,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You need more perspective.

119
00:08:47,087 --> 00:08:53,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective on the certain universal truths, universal reality of things, which is what dhamma is.

120
00:08:54,592 --> 00:08:58,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means, in other words, some people have little dust in their eyes, some people have more dust in their eyes.

121
00:08:58,835 --> 00:09:01,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, there is dust in their eyes that needs to be removed.

122
00:09:01,656 --> 00:09:02,697
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have too much.

123
00:09:02,997 --> 00:09:05,018
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have too much of the right perspective.

124
00:09:05,659 --> 00:09:06,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

125
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:09,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma is actually uprooting all of your putrūjana's perspectives.

126
00:09:10,803 --> 00:09:14,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they were not wrong, you would be free from suffering.

127
00:09:15,088 --> 00:09:16,469
[SPEAKER_01]: You would not need to practice.

128
00:09:18,732 --> 00:09:23,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, even as a prerequisite, yoniso manasikāra needs to be developed.

129
00:09:24,158 --> 00:09:24,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have it.

130
00:09:25,805 --> 00:09:31,787
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why often, like, I find it quite unfortunate, it's often translated and watered down.

131
00:09:32,087 --> 00:09:33,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's a wise considering.

132
00:09:34,788 --> 00:09:36,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It's appropriate attention.

133
00:09:37,029 --> 00:09:45,071
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all stuff that Putujana can relate to, in a sense of, oh yeah, I wisely consider and appropriately sort of approach my sensuality.

134
00:09:45,971 --> 00:09:46,092
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

135
00:09:46,232 --> 00:09:47,352
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are able

136
00:09:47,875 --> 00:09:51,637
[SPEAKER_01]: To approach centrality means there is no yoniso, manasikāra in you.

137
00:09:52,137 --> 00:09:53,738
[SPEAKER_00]: But the ayoniso is there.

138
00:09:53,858 --> 00:09:54,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Ayoniso is there.

139
00:09:54,638 --> 00:10:01,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, then, your idea of the right perspective is fully enveloped within the wrong perspective.

140
00:10:01,562 --> 00:10:04,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, everyone has a perspective.

141
00:10:04,103 --> 00:10:05,164
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always some perspective.

142
00:10:05,184 --> 00:10:16,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just whether it's going to be wrong or right, whether it's going to be limited or immeasurable, depending on... And certain direction, like a limited perspective, cannot result in immeasurable perspective.

143
00:10:17,541 --> 00:10:23,183
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to start developing the direction of immeasurable perspective, that can result in such perspectives.

144
00:10:23,243 --> 00:10:27,425
[SPEAKER_01]: But that perspective takes cultivation and development.

145
00:10:27,465 --> 00:10:29,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't happen to you.

146
00:10:29,146 --> 00:10:30,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot happen to you.

147
00:10:30,226 --> 00:10:34,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something you discern on account of basically being instructed.

148
00:10:34,528 --> 00:10:41,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yoniso, manasikāra and the right instruction are the necessary two prerequisites for arising of the right view.

149
00:10:42,692 --> 00:10:45,873
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need the right instruction, but you also need to have the right perspective.

150
00:10:47,379 --> 00:10:49,460
[SPEAKER_01]: So, putujana can engage in right perspective.

151
00:10:51,081 --> 00:10:53,182
[SPEAKER_01]: He can start doing it, so to speak.

152
00:10:54,242 --> 00:10:56,644
[SPEAKER_01]: And with the right instruction, he'll cease to be a putujana.

153
00:10:58,324 --> 00:11:00,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, right perspective is what is described before.

154
00:11:00,566 --> 00:11:13,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking at the universal characteristics of your current experience and not letting yourself be pulled or absorbed into the particulars of your observations, attention, techniques, sense objects and so on.

155
00:11:14,092 --> 00:11:18,333
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the beginning of the... well, that's at least the direction where you will develop the right perspective.

156
00:11:19,813 --> 00:11:30,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, the right perspective of anāgāmī, yoniso manasikāra of anāgāmī, is far superior to the possibility of yoniso manasikāra of kutujana, but it's the same direction.

157
00:11:30,855 --> 00:11:34,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if kutujana starts developing it, he will free himself from sensuality, for example.

158
00:11:37,816 --> 00:11:38,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's there.

159
00:11:38,697 --> 00:11:39,377
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the crossroads.

160
00:11:39,457 --> 00:11:40,397
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like left or right.

161
00:11:41,933 --> 00:11:47,096
[SPEAKER_01]: So to speak, or more actually, more accurately, it's more like the two planes.

162
00:11:47,216 --> 00:11:53,080
[SPEAKER_01]: One is that the horizontal plane of left, right, front and back, and yoniso manasikāra is the perpendicular plane.

163
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:57,182
[SPEAKER_01]: They would basically be surmounting any direction you take with your attention.

164
00:11:57,562 --> 00:11:59,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Perspective is there unchanged.

165
00:12:00,804 --> 00:12:01,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

166
00:12:02,465 --> 00:12:03,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Surmounts any... Yeah.

167
00:12:04,086 --> 00:12:06,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much like the nature of sati and mindfulness.

168
00:12:08,129 --> 00:12:08,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

169
00:12:08,329 --> 00:12:10,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is also why you can't have too much mindfulness.

170
00:12:10,250 --> 00:12:10,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

171
00:12:10,410 --> 00:12:10,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

172
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:11,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much the same thing.

173
00:12:16,408 --> 00:12:17,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's go to the next section.

174
00:12:18,630 --> 00:12:21,192
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, it taints to be abandoned by seeing.

175
00:12:23,734 --> 00:12:43,332
[SPEAKER_00]: It says here, Bhikkhu is an untaught ordinary person, a patujana, who has no regard for the noble ones, and is unskilled and undisciplined in their dhamma, who has no regard for true men and is unskilled and undisciplined in their dhamma, does not understand what things are fit for attention and what things are unfit for attention.

176
00:12:44,096 --> 00:12:46,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Since that is so, he attends to things unfit for attention.

177
00:12:47,297 --> 00:12:49,757
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't attend to things fit for attention.

178
00:12:50,498 --> 00:12:50,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:12:50,738 --> 00:13:03,381
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't understand what things, if attended to, will obscure the perspective, and what things, if attended to, will fortify the perspective.

180
00:13:03,701 --> 00:13:05,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has no perspective.

181
00:13:05,141 --> 00:13:06,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has no perspective.

182
00:13:06,101 --> 00:13:06,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a vicious circle.

183
00:13:07,002 --> 00:13:07,862
[SPEAKER_00]: He's locked in.

184
00:13:08,805 --> 00:13:12,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why he needs to start restraining beforehand, out of faith.

185
00:13:12,367 --> 00:13:24,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if he has no recognition of the value of sense restraint, and celibacy and so on, he needs to start doing it, because, see, at least, he unknowingly, he's not fueling the wrong perspective anymore.

186
00:13:25,296 --> 00:13:30,079
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a possibility that he's going to start seeing things in the right, right context.

187
00:13:30,319 --> 00:13:31,720
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he met, eh...

188
00:13:33,010 --> 00:13:36,433
[SPEAKER_00]: The true men, as it says, the noble ones, they were telling just that.

189
00:13:37,193 --> 00:13:40,516
[SPEAKER_00]: They were telling, you don't see things correctly, but do this.

190
00:13:40,656 --> 00:13:41,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Start restraining, yeah.

191
00:13:42,397 --> 00:13:45,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that is the perilous situation of putuja, as you said many times before.

192
00:13:48,282 --> 00:13:59,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know the escape, you don't see the truth, the right order, the right perspective, and because of that, the lack of perspective is getting worse.

193
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:00,871
[SPEAKER_00]: You think you have the right perspective.

194
00:14:00,951 --> 00:14:01,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

195
00:14:03,042 --> 00:14:09,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there has to be a perspective there, but it's just going to be wrong and more wrong.

196
00:14:10,308 --> 00:14:10,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

197
00:14:13,050 --> 00:14:16,753
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, what are things unfit for attention that he attends to?

198
00:14:17,233 --> 00:14:28,042
[SPEAKER_00]: They are things such, when he attends to them, the unarisen taint of sensual desire arises, and the arisen taint of sensual desire increases.

199
00:14:28,182 --> 00:14:28,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

200
00:14:28,743 --> 00:14:28,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

201
00:14:31,772 --> 00:14:33,614
[SPEAKER_00]: And the taint of being and the taint of ignorance.

202
00:14:42,141 --> 00:14:43,622
[SPEAKER_01]: We said that in other talks.

203
00:14:43,762 --> 00:14:57,374
[SPEAKER_01]: It connects the same when we spoke about the meditation and how to... how basically most people's meditation revolves around saying sīlabhātparamāsa, the virtue and duty, the fetter, the first fetter that impedes them.

204
00:14:57,634 --> 00:14:59,016
[SPEAKER_01]: It's related exactly to that.

205
00:14:59,036 --> 00:14:59,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

206
00:15:01,922 --> 00:15:15,365
[SPEAKER_01]: By not knowing, by not having perspective on what you need to do, what you need to attend to, you attend to right things wrongly, wrong things rightly, wrong things wrongly, but you can't attend to right things rightly, because you have no perspective on it.

207
00:15:16,646 --> 00:15:29,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eh... and that's what happens when... when people without such perspective then, eh... take on the practice and so on, but practice is not... it's not instructing them and teaching them how to look

208
00:15:30,117 --> 00:15:37,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, a developed perspective, in regard to what they're practicing, is just making them go through the motions of their practice.

209
00:15:37,567 --> 00:15:41,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, the precepts, and then do this, and then do this, and then do this, and then here is your mantra, and then do this.

210
00:15:42,293 --> 00:15:46,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more they do it, the less, the further away from getting the direction of the right perspective they are.

211
00:15:49,377 --> 00:15:51,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to do something.

212
00:15:51,717 --> 00:15:52,818
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says what you do.

213
00:15:53,498 --> 00:15:59,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint, virtue, well controlling your senses and so on.

214
00:15:59,879 --> 00:16:00,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you're doing.

215
00:16:00,999 --> 00:16:04,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, that is a factor to begin with, even if somebody's doing it rightly.

216
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:09,561
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you're also instructed to look at it, to gain perspective in regard to what you're doing.

217
00:16:10,361 --> 00:16:11,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just do this.

218
00:16:13,201 --> 00:16:16,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Do this and then understand why you're doing what you're doing.

219
00:16:16,847 --> 00:16:18,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop doing it.

220
00:16:18,970 --> 00:16:22,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop keeping the precepts in order to understand why you're keeping the precepts.

221
00:16:22,975 --> 00:16:28,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to keep keeping the precepts in order to be able to understand, eventually, the purpose of keeping the precepts.

222
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:30,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you won't be keeping them, in that sense.

223
00:16:30,504 --> 00:16:35,310
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be having to attend to them and do them, because now you understood their place and their nature and same.

224
00:16:35,610 --> 00:16:42,294
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be then doing any more meditation methods and techniques, because that was done before when there was no perspective.

225
00:16:42,374 --> 00:16:46,817
[SPEAKER_01]: You were hoping of going through the motions, will somehow bestow the knowledge upon you.

226
00:16:47,217 --> 00:16:51,460
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you realize, oh no, it's me gaining knowledge in regard to what I'm doing.

227
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:53,721
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to keeping the precepts, in regard to sensory strain.

228
00:16:54,021 --> 00:16:56,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, in regard to sitting quietly, just

229
00:16:57,273 --> 00:16:59,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm keeping the gaze in front of myself, not being distracted.

230
00:17:00,215 --> 00:17:05,637
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not doing it as a method or a technique that I will adhere to for 4 minutes 30 seconds.

231
00:17:06,278 --> 00:17:10,120
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm doing it so I can understand, like, the perspective behind.

232
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:13,021
[SPEAKER_01]: The universal characteristics of that.

233
00:17:14,562 --> 00:17:16,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Withdrawal, composure, mindfulness, all these things.

234
00:17:17,144 --> 00:17:18,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how understanding can arise.

235
00:17:19,425 --> 00:17:24,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you would overcome the first factor of doing things on account of practice.

236
00:17:24,488 --> 00:17:27,069
[SPEAKER_01]: And not seeing how they would be

237
00:17:27,593 --> 00:17:29,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Supporting the practice.

238
00:17:29,194 --> 00:17:31,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Having no direct knowledge of that.

239
00:17:32,157 --> 00:17:35,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just take on faith that being restrained and so on will help my practice.

240
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:39,583
[SPEAKER_01]: You take on faith that if I keep trying to meditate, I will get the right meditation.

241
00:17:42,450 --> 00:17:57,562
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless your meditation, your precepts and stuff are being reflected upon and you're trying to understand them as to why is this wholesome, why is this worthy of development, why is this... why would this result in freedom from suffering, you will never understand it.

242
00:17:58,043 --> 00:18:02,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no amount of do this, do this and keep these precepts would result in dhamma arising in you.

243
00:18:03,307 --> 00:18:07,751
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they said, the arahants themselves said that when they were at the first council.

244
00:18:08,915 --> 00:18:10,115
[SPEAKER_01]: What should we recite first?

245
00:18:10,255 --> 00:18:13,736
[SPEAKER_01]: The vinaya, the rules, or the sutta?

246
00:18:13,836 --> 00:18:17,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the sutta is the dhamma, but then say, but there's no dhamma without vinaya.

247
00:18:18,217 --> 00:18:19,257
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no dhamma without the rules.

248
00:18:19,797 --> 00:18:23,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rules are not the dhamma, but there is no dhamma without the basis of rules.

249
00:18:24,578 --> 00:18:25,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's recite the rules first.

250
00:18:26,359 --> 00:18:30,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they recited all the stories from the Vinaya Pitaka and then went into dhamma.

251
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:35,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rules are not the dhamma, but you wouldn't be able to

252
00:18:37,021 --> 00:18:39,522
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have any dhamma whatsoever if there are no rules established.

253
00:18:40,443 --> 00:18:45,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say rules, I mean virtue, precepts and sense restraint.

254
00:18:45,626 --> 00:18:46,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's all about that.

255
00:18:47,227 --> 00:18:48,507
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a calming fall.

256
00:18:48,687 --> 00:18:48,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

257
00:18:48,987 --> 00:18:58,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like you should... you could ask yourself, as somebody who wants to practice dhamma and keep the precepts, you ask yourself, okay, so do I understand this relationship between dhamma and the precepts?

258
00:18:59,133 --> 00:19:03,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do I think that my keeping of the precepts equates practicing of the dhamma?

259
00:19:03,475 --> 00:19:04,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't, actually.

260
00:19:05,627 --> 00:19:07,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping the precepts is one thing, practicing the dhamma is the other.

261
00:19:10,970 --> 00:19:12,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, do I understand this?

262
00:19:12,311 --> 00:19:12,491
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

263
00:19:12,551 --> 00:19:16,994
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't understand it, if you don't see clearly, you are taking the precepts, they are fettering you.

264
00:19:17,954 --> 00:19:24,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, sīla, bhātta, paramāsa, virtue, duty, the good things, the wholesome things, are a fetter.

265
00:19:25,059 --> 00:19:28,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have no perspective.

266
00:19:28,021 --> 00:19:28,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Ayoniso.

267
00:19:29,982 --> 00:19:31,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You're attending them without perspective.

268
00:19:32,566 --> 00:19:33,966
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if for a hundred years...

269
00:19:33,986 --> 00:19:40,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's good in terms of making merit, being virtuous and sense-restrained, but there will be no wisdom arising.

270
00:19:40,509 --> 00:19:50,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if for a hundred years you just keep the rules, but never invest effort into developing that perpendicular direction of... Oh, what's the... what's the context of these rules?

271
00:19:50,892 --> 00:19:54,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That I will not break, but let me understand this, the purpose.

272
00:19:54,453 --> 00:19:56,053
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do, then you don't need to hold it.

273
00:19:56,274 --> 00:19:57,454
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge has taken place.

274
00:20:00,224 --> 00:20:18,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why we say that, like, a noble person who is free from that fetter of Sīla Bhārata Paramāsa, even if they would sometimes, due to circumstances, break the rules, all they would need to do is just recognize that, and immediately, pretty much, their practice would be restored, when they see fault is fault, because they see fault is fault, they would understand it.

275
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:27,926
[SPEAKER_01]: While somebody who is not free from Sīla Bhārata Paramāsa, when they break the rules, it's even worse, because they had no perspective even with the rules.

276
00:20:29,263 --> 00:20:35,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they can't just repair it with an insight back on, because they had no insight to begin with.

277
00:20:35,786 --> 00:20:49,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to develop the direction of seeing the danger in the slightest fault, because, yeah, the slightest fault can undermine everything you build up that could have possibly helped you gain perspective on your own keeping the precepts, for example.

278
00:20:52,932 --> 00:20:54,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And we spoke about it in other talks.

279
00:20:55,226 --> 00:20:57,307
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize in the beginning you keep the precepts.

280
00:20:57,847 --> 00:21:02,108
[SPEAKER_01]: If you gain the perspective, it's not about me doing the precepts.

281
00:21:02,268 --> 00:21:05,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It's simply me not doing anything against the precepts.

282
00:21:06,409 --> 00:21:07,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't need to keep the precepts.

283
00:21:07,970 --> 00:21:09,290
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is not break them.

284
00:21:10,050 --> 00:21:10,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they're there.

285
00:21:11,190 --> 00:21:12,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that became your mode of behavior.

286
00:21:13,171 --> 00:21:14,531
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there were no rules in the beginning.

287
00:21:14,591 --> 00:21:16,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That was obvious to people who understood the dhamma.

288
00:21:17,205 --> 00:21:22,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why when that monk asked the Buddha... oh, there were too many, there were a hundred-something rules at the time.

289
00:21:22,509 --> 00:21:23,791
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, there's too many for me to keep.

290
00:21:23,871 --> 00:21:25,172
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't wrap my head around it.

291
00:21:25,652 --> 00:21:27,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, can you keep, what, three?

292
00:21:27,734 --> 00:21:30,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do anything rooted in greed, aversion and distraction.

293
00:21:31,597 --> 00:21:32,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything unwholesome.

294
00:21:32,998 --> 00:21:33,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Cultivate the wholesome.

295
00:21:34,019 --> 00:21:35,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do anything unwholesome.

296
00:21:35,020 --> 00:21:35,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate the wholesome.

297
00:21:35,801 --> 00:21:36,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So to speak.

298
00:21:37,622 --> 00:21:38,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, even just two rules.

299
00:21:38,702 --> 00:21:44,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he wouldn't... he knew, ah, anything that I can recognize, because he had perspective, as an wholesome, I'll not do it.

300
00:21:44,484 --> 00:21:53,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if there is no specific letter of the rule that bans you from doing it, you know that it's bad, because it's rooted in pre-diversion delusion, and you won't do it.

301
00:21:53,407 --> 00:21:56,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's your criteria, you can never break the rules.

302
00:21:56,789 --> 00:21:59,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, you can take it even on that fundamental level.

303
00:22:00,250 --> 00:22:06,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no rule against, I don't know, talking to people.

304
00:22:07,822 --> 00:22:08,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Something wholesome.

305
00:22:09,743 --> 00:22:16,785
[SPEAKER_01]: But you recognize that your motivation for doing so is rooted in you want to be distracted or or whatever.

306
00:22:18,706 --> 00:22:27,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Entertained, lustful, for whatever reason, you're engaging in that which is not unwholesome in its letter.

307
00:22:28,509 --> 00:22:35,351
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's unwholesome and you would have perspective on that, because your fundamental motivation is the fundamental perspective of your action.

308
00:22:36,514 --> 00:22:37,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what we said.

309
00:22:37,474 --> 00:22:39,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Every action is intention.

310
00:22:39,916 --> 00:22:41,156
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in your intention.

311
00:22:41,256 --> 00:22:42,417
[SPEAKER_01]: That determines the action.

312
00:22:43,117 --> 00:22:48,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if, like, publicly, like, you know, from the third-person point of view, it is nothing unwholesome.

313
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:57,323
[SPEAKER_01]: They're talking about books and suttas and tipiṭaka, but he does that internally for unwholesome reasons, or for not so wholesome reasons.

314
00:22:57,964 --> 00:23:05,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your criterion now, with your right perspective, is to never do things that are rooted in greed, aversion and distraction,

315
00:23:06,214 --> 00:23:08,016
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never, basically, be unwholesome.

316
00:23:08,056 --> 00:23:09,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never break the rules.

317
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:13,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not guided now by the letter.

318
00:23:13,502 --> 00:23:15,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but the rule says I can talk to people.

319
00:23:15,424 --> 00:23:16,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that doesn't matter.

320
00:23:17,005 --> 00:23:21,430
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like a very, eh, base criteria for people that have no criteria at all.

321
00:23:23,038 --> 00:23:24,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But you should strive higher than that.

322
00:23:25,499 --> 00:23:38,865
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to develop yoniso manasikāra properly, you would have perspective that wholesome unwholesome is determined by wholesome unwholesome intention behind those actions, and that's the criteria you follow, even if there is no formulated rule against it.

323
00:23:39,325 --> 00:23:46,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... if you start practicing like that, there are no loopholes in vinaya, because it's all either rooted in wholesome or unwholesome or neutral.

324
00:23:47,229 --> 00:23:49,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So yoniso manasikāra is pretty much the

325
00:23:51,338 --> 00:23:58,200
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamental thread that can take you from putujana state to being fully enlightened, if you cultivate it.

326
00:24:00,081 --> 00:24:12,444
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think we mentioned it before, it's like becoming that, you know, concerned for your own welfare and then recognizing the necessity of accepting responsibility for your actions, for your senses, for your intentions, for everything.

327
00:24:13,104 --> 00:24:14,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you're responsible,

328
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:19,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, when you feel responsible, well, you cannot afford to lose perspective.

329
00:24:19,442 --> 00:24:20,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this is really important.

330
00:24:21,183 --> 00:24:23,084
[SPEAKER_01]: I will consider everything before I commit to it.

331
00:24:23,264 --> 00:24:26,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Only when you are irresponsible, you just act out.

332
00:24:26,606 --> 00:24:28,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in mundane terms.

333
00:24:28,367 --> 00:24:29,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in colloquial sense.

334
00:24:30,348 --> 00:24:32,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Irresponsible people are people that don't really consider things.

335
00:24:32,990 --> 00:24:34,791
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't really have context.

336
00:24:35,031 --> 00:24:36,051
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't have perspective.

337
00:24:36,371 --> 00:24:37,572
[SPEAKER_01]: They just want this, don't want this.

338
00:24:37,612 --> 00:24:40,834
[SPEAKER_01]: They just irresponsibly act and then pretend it never happened or whatever else.

339
00:24:42,425 --> 00:24:55,375
[SPEAKER_01]: So, accepting responsibility for your own situation, even things you're not in control of, so to speak, it's it's better, because it can then prompt you in the direction of developing right perspective and right context, which is pretty much beginning of yonisumnasikāra.

340
00:24:56,116 --> 00:24:59,299
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, with the right instruction, you can get the right view and account of it.

341
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:10,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you wonder how it would feel like to do that, well, ask yourself, even in mundane terms, is responsibility pleasant or unpleasant?

342
00:25:10,648 --> 00:25:12,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Is responsibility burden?

343
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:13,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Or lighthearts are joy.

344
00:25:14,962 --> 00:25:15,402
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

345
00:25:15,622 --> 00:25:19,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's going to be heavier in the beginning, more unpleasant.

346
00:25:20,085 --> 00:25:25,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most of the life is about avoiding responsibility and getting the pleasure that covers up any sense of burden.

347
00:25:27,531 --> 00:25:38,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize that that's all optional, and you start accepting that burden of responsibility as a patujana, that's the basically precursor to the yoniso manasikāra.

348
00:25:38,279 --> 00:25:39,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, the mundane right view.

349
00:25:41,328 --> 00:25:47,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there are the results of my actions, there is, you know, there is giving, there is weight, there is significance of things.

350
00:25:47,653 --> 00:25:48,494
[SPEAKER_01]: I cannot just ignore.

351
00:25:48,814 --> 00:25:50,135
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the fact that I don't know.

352
00:25:51,116 --> 00:25:53,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also the truth for you to begin with.

353
00:25:53,818 --> 00:25:57,601
[SPEAKER_01]: That I'm not liberated, I'm not free from suffering.

354
00:25:57,701 --> 00:25:59,562
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have that wise perspective.

355
00:25:59,582 --> 00:25:59,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

356
00:26:00,083 --> 00:26:03,946
[SPEAKER_01]: So accepting the reality of your situation and responsibility for you being in it.

357
00:26:04,606 --> 00:26:09,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you keep yourself in whatever perspective you are in, right or wrong.

358
00:26:10,573 --> 00:26:14,974
[SPEAKER_01]: By cultivating wrong perspective, that's what's going to proliferate and diversify and become more.

359
00:26:15,815 --> 00:26:24,817
[SPEAKER_01]: By abstaining from it and kind of going against the pull of the wrong perspective and trying to develop that perpendicular right perspective, that will be on you again.

360
00:26:24,857 --> 00:26:26,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Through your own effort, you can.

361
00:26:27,918 --> 00:26:30,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, either way, you need to take responsibility.

